\section{Method}{

\enumerate{
\item \strong{Lithology classes.} Each lithology of a SIM 3340 map unit
(table NSALith: \code{LITH1}-\code{LITH5}, from general to specific, and \code{RANK})
is matched to an Olson class by its most specific term; when that term
is not a class, the next more general one is tried, and the match is
counted as \code{coarsened}. Olson split some lithologies by a modifier
(e.g. carbonaceous vs. non-carbonaceous shale) read from the unit
descriptions; SIM 3340 has no modifier field, so assign them with
\code{modifiers} (a table you fill in from the Description of Map Units,
one row per unit and lithology, with your rationale; start from
\code{\link[=rock_chem_crosswalk]{rock_chem_crosswalk()}}). Classes that are not rock types (water,
melange, landslide) and unmatched terms have no value: their share is
reported as \verb{_na_frac}.
\item \strong{Prevalence within a map unit.} SIM 3340 ranks each lithology by
how much of the unit it makes up: Major (at least 33\%), Minor (10-33\%),
Incidental (under 10\%); Indeterminate is taken as 10-100\%. The point
estimate uses the midpoint of each range, normalised within the unit;
the uncertainty draws a share uniformly within each range and
normalises again (\code{rank_shares} to change the ranges). Olson's
"prevalence weighting" is not documented in more detail than this.
\item \strong{Map unit value}: the weighted mean of its lithologies' values.
\strong{Catchment value}: the area-weighted mean over the map units in it
with a value.
}
}

\section{Uncertainty}{

Rock N within one lithology spans orders of magnitude (organic matter,
ammonium in micas and feldspars, metamorphic grade: high-grade rocks
have lost much of theirs), so a class value is a rough guess for any one
formation. Each draw (\code{n}, default 1000) combines:
\itemize{
\item \strong{the class value}: for each class, a shift on the log scale with SD
\code{sdlog / sqrt(n_records)} (how well the class mean is known), shared by
every unit with that class;
\item \strong{the formation}: for each map unit and lithology, a lognormal
deviation with SD \verb{formation_sd x sdlog}, mean-preserving, so that a
formation's real content can sit anywhere in its class's spread.
\code{formation_sd = 1} (default) attributes all of the spread among
records to differences between formations, which is conservative
(part of it is variation within a formation, which averages out over
a catchment); 0 keeps only the uncertainty of the class means;
\item \strong{the shares within each unit}, drawn within their rank ranges.
}

\code{sdlog} is the SD of log values among a class's source records in the
workbook. Classes with fewer than \code{min_n} records (or none: values
imputed or from one literature figure) get the 90th percentile of
\code{sdlog} among well-sampled classes of that element, so sparse classes
carry wide intervals. A unit shared by several catchments has the same
draw in all of them, so errors are correlated between catchments on the
same rocks, as they should be.

\verb{_pct} is the plug-in estimate (class values, rank midpoints), which is
what Olson's rasters give; \verb{_se}, \verb{_lo95} and \verb{_hi95} come from the
draws, which are right-skewed. Not included: map-boundary placement at
1:1,584,000 (see \code{\link[=positional_uncertainty]{positional_uncertainty()}}), misassigned lithologies,
and how far CONUS rocks represent Alaskan ones of the same name.

Flags (\verb{rock<el>_flag}): \code{no_value} (nothing characterised),
\code{high_na} (over 25\% of the mapped area without a value), \code{sparse_classes}
(over half the weight from classes with fewer than \code{min_n} records),
\code{coarsened_match} (over 25\% matched at a more general term),
\code{surficial_dominant} (over half the mapped area in unconsolidated units:
the value describes the deposits, not the bedrock beneath),
\code{partial_coverage} (under 95\% of the catchment mapped).

\strong{Surficial deposits.} SIM 3340, like Olson's maps, shows surficial
deposits where they were mapped and bedrock elsewhere.
\code{surficial = "exclude"} drops units made only of unconsolidated
lithologies (alluvium, glacial deposits, loess, ...) and averages over
the rest, as a sensitivity check; it is not a bedrock map (the rock under
the deposits is unknown). \code{rock_surficial_frac} reports their share.
}

